# 🍊 LeetCode 75 — Day 47: Rotting Oranges

**📌 Problem:** [994. Rotting Oranges](https://leetcode.com/problems/rotting-oranges/)
**🏷️ Topics:** Array, Matrix, BFS
**⚡ Difficulty:** Medium

---

## 🧠 Intuition

Rot spreads outward from *every* rotten orange simultaneously, one minute at a time — that's a **multi-source BFS**: instead of starting from a single point, seed the queue with every rotten orange at once, and let them all expand together, level by level (minute by minute).

---

## 🛠️ Approach

1. Scan the grid once: push every already-rotten orange (`2`) into the queue, and count every fresh orange (`1`) into `fresh`.
2. Run BFS while the queue isn't empty **and** there are still fresh oranges left:
   - Increment `time` for this new minute.
   - Process every orange currently in the queue (a full "level" = one minute's worth of spreading): for each of its 4 neighbors, if that neighbor is fresh (`1`), rot it (`set to 2`), push it into the queue, and decrement `fresh`.
3. Once the loop ends, check `fresh`: if any fresh oranges remain, they were unreachable — return `-1`. Otherwise, return `time`, the total minutes elapsed.

---

## ⏱️ Complexity

- **Time complexity:** `O(n * m)` — every cell in the grid is visited and processed at most once.
- **Space complexity:** `O(n * m)` — for the BFS queue in the worst case (a grid full of rotten oranges to start).

---

## 💻 Code

**Language/Format:** C++ — solved using LeetCode's OOP submission format (`class Solution` with a public method and a `valid()` helper), using multi-source BFS seeded with all initially-rotten oranges.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:

    int x[4] = {-1, 1, 0, 0};
    int y[4] = {0, 0, -1, 1};

    bool valid(int i, int j, int n, int m) {

        if (i < 0 || i >= n || j < 0 || j >= m)
            return false;

        return true;
    }

    int orangesRotting(vector<vector<int>>& grid) {

        int n = grid.size();
        int m = grid[0].size();

        queue<pair<int, int>> q;

        int fresh = 0;
        int time = 0;

        // Put all rotten oranges into queue
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {
                if (grid[i][j] == 2) {
                    q.push({i, j});
                }
                else if (grid[i][j] == 1) {
                    fresh++;
                }
            }
        }

        // BFS
        while (!q.empty() && fresh > 0) {

            time++;

            int s = q.size();

            while (s--) {

                pair<int, int> p = q.front();
                q.pop();

                int r = p.first;
                int c = p.second;

                for (int k = 0; k < 4; k++) {

                    int row = r + x[k];
                    int col = c + y[k];

                    if (valid(row, col, n, m) &&
                        grid[row][col] == 1) {

                        grid[row][col] = 2;
                        q.push({row, col});
                        fresh--;
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        if (fresh > 0)
            return -1;

        return time;
    }
};
```

---

## 🔑 Key Takeaway

When something spreads from multiple starting points at the same time (rot, fire, infection, flood-fill from several seeds), seed the BFS queue with *all* of them upfront rather than running separate BFS calls — the level-by-level structure of BFS then naturally models simultaneous spreading, with each level representing one full time unit.

